    <system:String x:Key="Metric.AircraftRate">AIRCRAFT VERTICAL RATE</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Metric.AircraftRateSubline">vertical speed at contact</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Help.AircraftRate">How fast the aircraft itself was moving vertically at contact. Negative means descending. This value is calculated from the aircraft's motion, without runway slope.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Help.LandingFeelName">Landing feel classification information</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Metric.SurfaceClosure">SURFACE CLOSURE RATE</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Help.SurfaceClosure">The touchdown rate reported by MSFS. It describes how quickly the aircraft and the surface came together, so runway slope and aircraft rotation can make it differ from Aircraft Vertical Rate.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Help.SurfaceDelta">The difference between Surface Closure Rate and Aircraft Vertical Rate. It shows how much the simulator's touchdown value differs from the aircraft's own vertical motion.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Metric.SurfaceDelta">Surface closure delta</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Help.Terrain">The part of the difference caused by the local surface rising or falling under the aircraft. A rising surface increases closure; a falling surface reduces it.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Metric.Terrain">Terrain component</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Help.Unresolved">What remains after the older, terrain-only comparison. It combines timing, flare, rotation and sampling effects; it is not the error of the Experimental Closure Reconstruction below.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Metric.Unresolved">Unresolved component</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Metric.PeakG150">PEAK G · 150 MS</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Help.PeakG150">The highest vertical G-load during the first 150 milliseconds after contact. It captures the immediate impact and is a peak value, not an average.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Help.PeakG2">The highest vertical G-load during the first two seconds after contact. It can reveal a later gear reaction, bounce or second impact that the 150 ms value does not include.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Metric.PeakG2">Peak G over 2 seconds</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Metric.Ias">IAS AT CONTACT</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Metric.Indicated">indicated</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Help.Ias">Indicated airspeed at contact. This is the speed shown on the aircraft's airspeed indicator, not its speed over the ground.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Metric.PitchBank">PITCH · BANK</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Help.PitchBank">The aircraft's nose-up or nose-down angle and left or right bank at contact. Together they show the aircraft's attitude and any side-to-side asymmetry.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Help.Aoa">Angle of attack at contact: the angle between the wing and the airflow. It is different from pitch because the aircraft can also be climbing or descending through the air.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Metric.GroundSpeed">GROUND SPEED</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Metric.AtContact">at contact</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Help.GroundSpeed">The aircraft's speed over the ground at contact. Wind can make it different from IAS.</system:String>

    <system:String x:Key="Recon.Title">EXPERIMENTAL CLOSURE RECONSTRUCTION</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Recon.Help">MSFS reports one Surface Closure Rate when the aircraft touches down. This panel tries to reproduce that number independently from the recorded aircraft motion, runway and landing-gear data. It is a cross-check and never replaces the value reported by MSFS above.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Recon.Model">CALCULATION METHOD</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Recon.ModelHelp">The name of the calculation method used for this landing. The method is kept fixed so different landings remain comparable; it is not adjusted to make this particular result look better.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Recon.ModeledClosure">MODELED CLOSURE</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Recon.ModeledClosureHelp">The Surface Closure Rate independently estimated from the recorded telemetry. Compare it with the MSFS value above: the closer they are, the more of the touchdown reading the model has explained.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Recon.RawMinusModel">RAW − MODEL</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Recon.RawMinusModelHelp">The difference between the Surface Closure Rate reported by MSFS and the independent estimate. Zero is a perfect match; a value inside the stated Uncertainty means the model explains the reading as closely as the recorded data allows.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Recon.Components">MODELED COMPONENTS</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Recon.ComponentsHelp">The three parts that add up to Modeled Closure. Inertial is the aircraft moving down, terrain is the runway rising or falling, and pitch is the main gear moving vertically as the aircraft rotates. Inertial can differ from Aircraft Vertical Rate because the MSFS touchdown value appears to describe a slightly earlier moment.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Recon.Uncertainty">UNCERTAINTY</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Recon.UncertaintyHelp">The expected tolerance of the reconstruction based on recorded test landings. It is normally ±10 fpm, or ±15 fpm for harder cases such as a bounce, less precise contact timing, three main gear groups or configured multi-bogie gear. A multi-bogie aircraft using telemetry-derived geometry retains a conservative ±50 fpm band. This tolerance belongs to the model, not to the MSFS value.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Recon.Geometry">GEOMETRY SOURCE</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Recon.GeometryHelp">How the app learned the distance between the main gear and the point whose motion MSFS reports. A readable flight model is preferred; telemetry is the fallback for encrypted or unavailable configurations. Quality shows how clearly telemetry exposed that geometry; it is not a probability.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Recon.UnavailableTitle">RECONSTRUCTION UNAVAILABLE FOR THIS RECORD</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Recon.UnavailableBody">The app does not have enough recorded data to make this reconstruction honestly. The landing may be an older record, the gear sequence may be unclear, or there may be too few useful samples. The measured values above remain available and are not changed.</system:String>

